window.AILETMOCK={"n":5,"title":"Mock Test 5","theme":"Privilege, sedition and contempt","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"Parliamentary privilege, sedition, electoral reforms, tribal rights, contempt and Ayodhya in constitutional law. Seven more branches of law and contemporary legal GK complete the paper.","minutes":120,"neg":0.25,"sec":["Constitutional Law","Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","International Law and Human Rights","Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK"],"q":[{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Parliamentary Privilege","q":"In Raja Ram Pal v. Hon'ble Speaker, Lok Sabha (2007), the Supreme Court, in upholding the expulsion of MPs in the 'cash-for-query' case, held that:","o":["parliamentary privileges are wholly immune from any judicial scrutiny","expulsion is permitted only for criminal conviction in a court of law","limited judicial review of the exercise of privilege is permitted","expulsion of a member is permitted only by the President on advice"],"a":2,"e":"A five-judge bench held that the power to expel is a part of the privileges of the House, but that courts may review the exercise of the power on grounds such as illegality and violation of fundamental rights."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Sedition","q":"In Kedar Nath Singh v. State of Bihar (1962), the Supreme Court upheld the validity of the sedition law (Section 124A IPC) but limited it to acts involving:","o":["incitement to violence or a tendency to cause public disorder","any criticism of the Government established by law in India","any disaffection expressed against a public servant in his office","any speech that causes annoyance to a section of the population"],"a":0,"e":"The Court distinguished between strong criticism of the Government, which is protected by Article 19(1)(a), and incitement to violence. The Supreme Court in 2022 kept Section 124A in abeyance, and the BNS has replaced it with Section 152."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Free Speech","q":"In Imran Pratapgarhi v. State of Gujarat (2025), while quashing an FIR over a poem posted on social media, the Supreme Court stressed that:","o":["poems and creative works fall outside the protection of Article 19(1)(a)","courts must protect free expression even when the views are widely disliked","free expression yields to any speech that offends a section of society","police must register an FIR at once on every complaint of offence by speech"],"a":1,"e":"The Court observed that the guarantee under Article 19(1)(a) must be upheld, and that the police should be sensitised to the constitutional limits on restricting speech under Article 19(2). (Verify the details before the exam.)"},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Electoral Reforms","q":"In Public Interest Foundation v. Union of India (2018), the Constitution Bench held that disqualification of a legislator:","o":["can be added by the Election Commission through its own orders at will","cannot be added by the Court on framing of charges, and is for Parliament","follows automatically on the framing of charges for any serious offence","follows automatically on the filing of a police charge-sheet in the court"],"a":1,"e":"The Court asked Parliament to frame a strong law to prevent criminalisation of politics, and directed candidates to disclose their criminal antecedents prominently, and parties to publish these details."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Armed Forces","q":"In Secretary, Ministry of Defence v. Babita Puniya (2020), the Supreme Court directed that women officers in the Army:","o":["be granted permanent commission on par with their male counterparts","be granted permanent commission only in the legal and medical branches","be granted permanent commission only in non-combat training establishments","remain on short service commission without any option of an extension"],"a":0,"e":"The Court rejected the stereotypes about women's physiological limitations and domestic obligations as a basis for denying equal opportunity under Articles 14, 15 and 16."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Life","q":"In Parmanand Katara v. Union of India (1989), the Supreme Court held that every doctor, whether in a government hospital or otherwise, has a professional obligation to:","o":["transfer every accident victim to the nearest government hospital first","wait for the police to complete formalities before any treatment starts","provide treatment only after the patient has deposited the fees required","give immediate medical aid, without waiting for legal formalities"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that preservation of life is of paramount importance under Article 21, and the legal formalities such as police intimation should not delay emergency treatment. This principle supports the Good Samaritan guidelines."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Governors","q":"In State of Punjab v. Principal Secretary to the Governor of Punjab (2023), the Supreme Court held that a Governor who withholds assent to a Bill under Article 200:","o":["may keep it pending indefinitely, with no duty to act on it","must return the Bill to the legislature for reconsideration","may refer it to the Supreme Court for an opinion under Article 143","may veto it permanently, without returning it to the legislature"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the first proviso to Article 200 requires that when assent is withheld, the Bill must be returned. The Governor is a constitutional head and cannot veto a Bill by sitting over it."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Finance","q":"Which Article of the Constitution deals with the power of a State to borrow upon the security of the Consolidated Fund of the State?","o":["Article 292","Article 266","Article 267","Article 293"],"a":3,"e":"Article 292 deals with borrowing by the Government of India. Article 293 permits States to borrow within India, and needs the Union's consent if any loan of the Union is still outstanding. Kerala's challenge to borrowing limits has been an important federal dispute."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Tribal Rights","q":"In Samatha v. State of Andhra Pradesh (1997), the Supreme Court held that, in Scheduled Areas, the transfer of tribal land to non-tribals for mining:","o":["is barred, and the State cannot lease tribal land to private persons","is allowed, if the Governor has issued a notification under Article 244","is allowed, if the State Government has granted its prior consent","is allowed, if the lessee pays full market compensation to the tribals"],"a":0,"e":"The Court read the Fifth Schedule, Article 21 and the directive principles to protect tribal rights over land and natural resources, and treated the lease to private parties as void."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Judicial Appointments","q":"In Lok Prahari v. Union of India (2021), the Supreme Court permitted the appointment of ad hoc judges in High Courts under Article 224A to deal with:","o":["election petitions arising out of State Legislative Assembly polls","the large backlog of pending cases, subject to guidelines","urgent constitutional questions referred by the President of India","matters in which regular judges have a personal conflict of interest"],"a":1,"e":"Article 224A allows the Chief Justice of a High Court, with the President's consent, to request a retired judge to sit and act as a judge of that High Court. The Court laid down the conditions in which this may be done."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Contempt","q":"In Re: Prashant Bhushan (2020), the Supreme Court, after holding him guilty of criminal contempt for his tweets, imposed a fine of:","o":["₹1,000","₹1","₹10,000","₹1 lakh"],"a":1,"e":"The Court gave him the option of paying the fine by a fixed date, failing which he would face three months' simple imprisonment and a bar from practising for three years."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Ayodhya","q":"In M. Siddiq v. Mahant Suresh Das (2019), the Supreme Court invoked Article 142 to direct the allotment of how much alternative land to the Sunni Central Waqf Board for a mosque?","o":["two acres","three acres","five acres","ten acres"],"a":2,"e":"The Court awarded the disputed 2.77 acres to the deity (Ram Lalla Virajman) for a temple, to be managed through a trust framed by the Central Government, and held that the demolition of the mosque in 1992 was a violation of the law."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Official Language","q":"Under the Official Languages Act, 1963, as amended in 1967, English may continue to be used for official purposes of the Union:","o":["only until 1965, after which its use must stop completely","only until Parliament decides by a simple majority resolution of it","only in Union Territories, and not in any State's official work","until the legislatures of all non-Hindi States resolve to stop it"],"a":3,"e":"The practical effect is that English continues indefinitely as an associate official language, alongside Hindi in Devanagari script, which is the official language under Article 343."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Special Provisions","q":"Which Article of the Constitution contains special provisions with respect to the State of Nagaland?","o":["Article 371C","Article 371A","Article 371B","Article 371G"],"a":1,"e":"Article 371A (13th Amendment, 1962) provides that Acts of Parliament in respect of religious or social practices of the Nagas, Naga customary law and ownership of land do not apply unless the Assembly so decides. Article 371G concerns Mizoram."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"New States","q":"In Babulal Parante v. State of Bombay (1960), the Supreme Court held that, under Article 3, the views of the State Legislature on a Bill affecting the State's boundaries are:","o":["binding on Parliament, which cannot pass the Bill without its consent","binding on Parliament only if two-thirds of the members agree","irrelevant, since the President need not refer the Bill to the legislature","to be ascertained by the President, but are not binding on Parliament"],"a":3,"e":"Parliament may, therefore, reorganise States even over the objection of the State concerned, which reflects the indestructible Union of destructible States doctrine, as in the creation of Telangana in 2014."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Chief Minister","q":"In S.R. Chaudhuri v. State of Punjab (2001), the Supreme Court held that a person who is not a member of the State Legislature:","o":["cannot be repeatedly appointed Chief Minister for successive six-month terms","can be appointed Chief Minister for any number of successive terms","can be appointed only with prior approval of the High Court concerned","cannot be appointed Chief Minister under any circumstance of the Constitution"],"a":0,"e":"Article 164(4) permits a non-member Minister to hold office for six months, but a repeated appointment of the same person without his getting elected would be an abuse of the constitutional provision."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Governor's Immunity","q":"Article 361(2) provides that, during his term of office, no criminal proceedings can be instituted or continued against:","o":["the President and the Governor of a State","the Chief Justice of India and the Chief Election Commissioner","the Vice-President and the Speaker of the Lok Sabha","the Prime Minister and the Chief Ministers of the States"],"a":0,"e":"The immunity under Article 361 is personal and does not bar judicial review of the actions of the executive. Rameshwar Prasad (2006) held that the immunity does not extend to the acts being challenged as mala fide."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Election Commission","q":"In Mohinder Singh Gill v. Chief Election Commissioner (1978), the Supreme Court held that the powers of the Election Commission under Article 324:","o":["are absolute and free from the requirements of natural justice","are plenary in areas not covered by law, but must follow natural justice","are wholly exercisable by the Union Government acting through the Commission","are limited strictly to the statutes enacted by Parliament on elections"],"a":1,"e":"Article 324 operates in areas left unoccupied by legislation, but the Commission has to act fairly and cannot act arbitrarily, and its orders are subject to judicial review."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Civil Services","q":"In Union of India v. Tulsiram Patel (1985), the Constitution Bench held that the second proviso to Article 311(2), which dispenses with an inquiry before dismissal, is valid where:","o":["holding an inquiry is not reasonably practicable, for recorded reasons","the Government wishes to save the time and cost of holding an inquiry","the employee has served for less than five years in the service","the Minister concerned has approved the dismissal order in writing"],"a":0,"e":"The Court upheld the provision but held that the reasons for dispensing with the inquiry must be recorded in writing, and the decision is subject to judicial review."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Double Jeopardy","q":"In Maqbool Hussain v. State of Bombay (1953), the Supreme Court held that the protection against double jeopardy in Article 20(2) applies only if the earlier proceeding was:","o":["a prosecution before a court of law or a judicial tribunal","any departmental inquiry held against a Government servant","any administrative inquiry held by a customs officer","any preventive proceeding under the security laws"],"a":0,"e":"Confiscation of gold by the customs authorities was held not to be a prosecution before a court, so a later prosecution under the Foreign Exchange Act was not barred."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Privacy","q":"In Kharak Singh v. State of U.P. (1962), the Supreme Court held that:","o":["all forms of police surveillance of a suspect are unconstitutional","night-time domiciliary visits by police violate Article 21","domiciliary visits at night are valid, as no right is infringed","the right to privacy is expressly guaranteed as a fundamental right"],"a":1,"e":"The majority struck down the regulation permitting night-time domiciliary visits, but held that shadowing by the police did not violate Article 19(1)(d), and that privacy was not a fundamental right, a view overruled in Puttaswamy (2017)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Shelter","q":"In Chameli Singh v. State of U.P. (1996), the Supreme Court held that the right to life under Article 21 includes:","o":["the right to a house of a size that is fixed by the Government","the right to be rehoused anywhere in the country by the Union","the right to free land from the State for every landless citizen","the right to shelter, as an essential part of a dignified life"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that shelter includes adequate living space, safe and decent structure, clean surroundings and access to basic amenities, and read it along with the Directive Principles."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Mandatory Death Sentence","q":"In Mithu v. State of Punjab (1983), the Supreme Court struck down Section 303 of the IPC, which provided:","o":["a mandatory life sentence for all persons convicted of murder","a mandatory fine for the offence of culpable homicide","a mandatory death sentence for murder committed by a life convict","a mandatory death sentence for every offence of abetting suicide"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that a mandatory death sentence, leaving no discretion to the court to consider the circumstances, violates Articles 14 and 21. The BNS has no mandatory death penalty of this kind."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Preventive Detention","q":"In A.K. Roy v. Union of India (1982), the Supreme Court upheld the National Security Act, 1980 and held that a detenu:","o":["has no right to a statement of the grounds for his detention","can be detained for any period without an Advisory Board review","has no right to be represented by a lawyer before the Advisory Board","has a right to be represented by a lawyer before the Advisory Board"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that the Advisory Board procedure is not a judicial proceeding, though the Government may allow legal assistance in special cases, and that the grounds of detention must be communicated."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under Section 65(2) of the BNS, rape of a woman under twelve years of age is punishable with:","o":["rigorous imprisonment of 20 years or more, up to life, or death","imprisonment for 7 years, which may extend to 10 years, and fine","imprisonment for life only, and no punishment of death is prescribed","rigorous imprisonment of not less than 10 years, which may extend to 14 years"],"a":0,"e":"Life imprisonment here means imprisonment for the remainder of the person's natural life. Section 65(1) deals with a girl under sixteen."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under Section 124 of the BNS, voluntarily causing grievous hurt by the use of acid is punishable with imprisonment of:","o":["not less than fourteen years, which may extend to death, and fine","not less than seven years, which may extend to ten years only","not less than three years, which may extend to five years, and fine","not less than ten years, which may extend to life, and fine"],"a":3,"e":"The fine must be just and reasonable to meet the medical expenses of the victim. Throwing or attempting to throw acid is separately punishable under Section 124(2)."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Section 398 of the BNSS requires every State Government to:","o":["set up a witness testimony recording centre in every district court","prepare and notify a Witness Protection Scheme","create a separate fund for compensating all victims of road accidents","establish a special police force for protection of senior judges"],"a":1,"e":"The provision gives statutory form to the scheme approved by the Supreme Court in Mahender Chawla v. Union of India (2018) for the protection of witnesses against threats."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Handcuffing","q":"In Prem Shankar Shukla v. Delhi Administration (1980), the Supreme Court held that handcuffing of a prisoner is:","o":["unlawful in every circumstance, with no exception being permitted at all","prima facie inhuman and unreasonable, justified only in rare circumstances","lawful in every case where an accused is arrested for a serious offence","lawful whenever the police officer in charge of the case considers it so"],"a":1,"e":"Handcuffs may be used only where there is a clear and present danger of escape, which has to be recorded in writing, and subject to the Magistrate's approval. The BNSS now permits handcuffing in specified serious offences at the time of arrest."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Evidence","q":"On the testimony of an approver (accomplice), the law of evidence provides that:","o":["an approver's testimony is always inadmissible against a co-accused","an approver's testimony needs corroboration only in murder cases alone","a conviction on his bare word is valid, though prudence seeks corroboration","a conviction on the uncorroborated testimony of an approver is illegal"],"a":2,"e":"An accomplice is a competent witness, but courts as a rule of prudence insist on corroboration in material particulars. The principle is now found in the BSA."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BSA","q":"Facts which, though not in issue, form part of the same transaction (the doctrine of res gestae) are made relevant by which section of the BSA?","o":["Section 5","Section 4","Section 2","Section 3"],"a":1,"e":"Section 4 of the BSA corresponds to Section 6 of the Evidence Act, 1872. Section 5 deals with facts that are the occasion, cause or effect of facts in issue."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Default Bail","q":"In Ritu Chhabria v. Union of India (2023), the Supreme Court held that the investigating agency:","o":["must file the final charge-sheet within seven days of the arrest","cannot file an incomplete charge-sheet to defeat the right to default bail","is barred from further investigation after the filing of a charge-sheet","may file an incomplete charge-sheet to prevent default bail at any stage"],"a":1,"e":"The right to default bail under Section 167(2) CrPC (now Section 187 BNSS) arises when the investigation is not completed within 60 or 90 days, and is part of Article 21. Further investigation may continue after the filing of the charge-sheet."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Anticipatory Bail","q":"The principles governing anticipatory bail, rejecting rigid conditions and emphasising the court's discretion, were laid down by the Constitution Bench in:","o":["Gurbaksh Singh Sibbia v. State of Punjab (1980)","Sushila Aggarwal v. State (NCT of Delhi) (2020)","Joginder Kumar v. State of U.P. (1994)","Arnesh Kumar v. State of Bihar (2014)"],"a":0,"e":"Sibbia held that Section 438 should be given a wide interpretation in light of Article 21, and the power should not be hedged in by conditions not found in the section. Sushila Aggarwal revisited the question of the time limit."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Quashing","q":"The categories of cases in which the High Court may quash an FIR in exercise of its inherent powers (now Section 528 BNSS) were enumerated in:","o":["Rupan Deol Bajaj v. K.P.S. Gill (1995)","State of Haryana v. Bhajan Lal (1992)","State of Bihar v. P.P. Sharma (1991)","Kartar Singh v. State of Punjab (1994)"],"a":1,"e":"The Court listed seven illustrative categories, such as allegations that do not constitute an offence, or are absurd or inherently improbable, or where the proceeding is manifestly mala fide."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Compounding","q":"In Gian Singh v. State of Punjab (2012), the Supreme Court held that the High Court can quash criminal proceedings on the basis of a settlement, even in non-compoundable offences, but not in:","o":["offences arising out of a matrimonial dispute between a husband and wife","offences that arise out of civil, family or personal disputes","heinous and serious offences such as murder, rape and dacoity","offences relating to property or involving a commercial transaction"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that the inherent power under Section 482 is distinct from the power to compound offences, but it should be exercised with caution in the interest of justice."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Matrimonial Offences","q":"In Social Action Forum for Manav Adhikar v. Union of India (2018), the Supreme Court:","o":["held that arrests under Section 498A are barred in all circumstances","held that Section 498A of the IPC is unconstitutional and void","set aside the direction for Family Welfare Committees to scrutinise complaints","ordered that no FIR can be lodged under Section 498A without a court's leave"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that the earlier direction in Rajesh Sharma (2017) amounted to judicial legislation, and the safeguards in Arnesh Kumar and Section 41 CrPC remain applicable."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Sexual Offences","q":"In State of Jharkhand v. Shailendra Kumar Rai (2022), the Supreme Court held that the 'two-finger test' on rape survivors:","o":["is permitted if the survivor is above eighteen years of age at the time","is a valid way of establishing consent in every rape prosecution","is permitted when a Magistrate has given a written order for the test","violates privacy and dignity, and a doctor who does it commits misconduct"],"a":3,"e":"The test was earlier described as unscientific and violative of dignity in Lillu v. State of Haryana (2013), and the Court said that the survivor's sexual history is not relevant to whether she was raped."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"POCSO","q":"In Attorney General for India v. Satish (2021), the Supreme Court held that for the offence of 'sexual assault' under Section 7 of the POCSO Act:","o":["skin-to-skin contact is not needed, as sexual intent in the touch is enough","the act must result in a physical injury to the child concerned","the offender must be an adult, and not another child or adolescent","skin-to-skin contact is essential, and contact through clothing is excluded"],"a":0,"e":"The Court set aside the Bombay High Court's narrow reading, which would have defeated the object of protecting children from sexual offences."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Legal Aid","q":"In Khatri v. State of Bihar (1981), arising from the Bhagalpur blindings, the Supreme Court held that the State must provide free legal aid to an indigent accused:","o":["only at the stage of an appeal before the High Court concerned","from the first production before the Magistrate, under Article 21","only at the stage of the trial before the Sessions Court","only if the accused has made a written request before the trial"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the right to free legal services is implicit in the guarantee of fair procedure under Article 21, and that Magistrates must inform the accused of this right."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Contract","q":"Under Section 26 of the Indian Contract Act, an agreement in restraint of the marriage of any person, other than a minor, is:","o":["void","voidable","valid, if reasonable","valid, if partial"],"a":0,"e":"The provision reflects the public policy that marriage should be free. Section 27 deals with restraint of trade and Section 28 with restraint of legal proceedings."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Wagering Agreements","q":"In Gherulal Parakh v. Mahadeodas Maiya (1959), the Supreme Court held that a wagering agreement under Section 30 of the Contract Act is:","o":["unlawful, so all collateral transactions to it are also void","valid, so a suit can be brought to recover the winnings in court","voidable, so either party may avoid it before the due date arrives","void but not unlawful, so collateral transactions are enforceable"],"a":3,"e":"Section 30 declares wagers void and bars suits to recover winnings, but it does not make the wager forbidden by law, so a collateral agreement (for example, to lend money to pay a wager) is not rendered void under Section 23."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Unconscionable Contracts","q":"In Central Inland Water Transport Corporation v. Brojo Nath Ganguly (1986), the Supreme Court held that an unfair and unreasonable contract term, imposed by a party with superior bargaining power, is:","o":["void under Section 23 of the Contract Act, as opposed to public policy","voidable, but only at the option of the party that has imposed it","valid, if the contract has been approved by a court of competent jurisdiction","valid, because the weaker party has signed it of his own free will"],"a":0,"e":"The Court struck down a service rule that allowed termination on three months' notice, applying the principle of unconscionability to standard form contracts and holding that an unfair term infringes Article 14."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Vicarious Liability","q":"The principal test for distinguishing a 'servant' from an 'independent contractor' for the purposes of vicarious liability is the:","o":["control test, asking whether the employer controls how the work is done","place test, which asks where the work is physically carried out","payment test, which asks how the person is paid for the work done","duration test, which asks how long the person has been engaged for"],"a":0,"e":"An employer is generally liable for torts of a servant committed in the course of employment but not for those of an independent contractor, though courts also use the organisation and multiple tests."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Torts","q":"In Entick v. Carrington (1765), the court held that a search of a person's papers and premises by the executive's messengers was:","o":["lawful, as long as the search was carried out in the daytime","lawful, since a Secretary of State had issued the warrant","lawful, as State necessity justifies any search of a seditious writer","unlawful in the absence of legal authority for the search"],"a":3,"e":"The case is a foundation of the rule that the State's powers must have a legal basis, and of the protection of property and privacy against arbitrary intrusion."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Defences in Tort","q":"The defence of 'act of God' to an action in tort, as an exception to strict liability, was recognised in:","o":["Donoghue v. Stevenson (1932)","Wagon Mound (No. 1) (1961)","Nichols v. Marsland (1876)","Rylands v. Fletcher (1868)"],"a":2,"e":"In Nichols v. Marsland, an extraordinary rainfall caused artificial lakes to burst and wash away bridges, and the defendant was held not liable since the damage was caused by an act of God which no human foresight could have anticipated."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"False Imprisonment","q":"In Bird v. Jones (1845), the court held that preventing a person from going in one direction, while leaving him free to go in another, is:","o":["not false imprisonment, as there must be a total restraint","malicious prosecution, if the intention was to cause harm","trespass to land, but not false imprisonment of the person","false imprisonment, even if only a partial restraint is imposed"],"a":0,"e":"False imprisonment requires a complete deprivation of liberty, and a partial obstruction, where a reasonable means of exit exists, does not suffice."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Estoppel in Property Law","q":"Under Section 43 of the Transfer of Property Act (feeding the grant by estoppel), where a person fraudulently or erroneously represents that he is authorised to transfer property and transfers it for consideration, the transferee:","o":["has no right to the property, as the transfer was void ab initio","acquires the property in every case, without any option to rescind it","may claim the property at his option if the transferor later gains an interest","may claim only a refund of the consideration, with no right in the property"],"a":2,"e":"The provision protects a transferee who was misled, and the transfer operates on any interest which the transferor acquires subsequently, unless the contract is rescinded."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Registration","q":"Under Section 17 of the Registration Act, 1908, a non-testamentary instrument creating a right in immovable property of the value of ₹100 or more must be:","o":["notarised before a Magistrate","attested by a gazetted officer","compulsorily registered","stamped with a revenue stamp only"],"a":2,"e":"Section 49 provides that an unregistered document required to be registered is not admissible as evidence of a transaction affecting the property, although it may be used for collateral purposes."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Restitution of Conjugal Rights","q":"In Saroj Rani v. Sudarshan Kumar Chadha (1984), the Supreme Court, as regards Section 9 of the Hindu Marriage Act (restitution of conjugal rights):","o":["struck it down as discriminatory against women under Article 15 itself","upheld its validity, and overruled the Andhra Pradesh High Court's view","referred it to a larger bench without deciding its validity at all","struck it down as an infringement of the right to privacy and liberty"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the remedy is a matrimonial one aimed at preserving the marriage, and not a violation of bodily integrity, rejecting the reasoning in T. Sareetha v. T. Venkata Subbaiah (1983)."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Muslim Women","q":"In Danial Latifi v. Union of India (2001), the Supreme Court upheld the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act, 1986 and held that the husband's liability:","o":["is limited strictly to the payment of maintenance during the iddat period","extends to a fair provision for her whole future, beyond the iddat","does not exist, since she is entitled to nothing on a divorce at all","extends only to returning the mehr, and nothing further to her"],"a":1,"e":"The Court read the Act so as to uphold its validity under Articles 14, 15 and 21, requiring the provision to be made within the iddat period but to cover her entire future."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Hindu Law","q":"Under Mitakshara law, a son acquires an interest in the coparcenary property:","o":["on partition of the family","on the death of his father","on his marriage","by birth, as a coparcener"],"a":3,"e":"Since the Hindu Succession (Amendment) Act, 2005, daughters are also coparceners by birth with the same rights (Vineeta Sharma, 2020)."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Public Contracts","q":"In Tata Cellular v. Union of India (1994), the Supreme Court held that judicial review of the Government's decisions in awarding contracts and tenders:","o":["extends to the merits of the decision, as in an appeal before the court","is allowed only where a rival bidder has filed a complaint on its terms","is barred altogether, since contracts are within the executive's domain","is limited to the decision-making process and not the merits of the decision"],"a":3,"e":"Courts examine arbitrariness, irrationality, mala fides and procedural impropriety, but give wide latitude to the executive in commercial decisions, and this principle has been followed in later tender cases."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Law and Morality","q":"In 'The Enforcement of Morals', Lord Devlin argued that:","o":["society may use law to enforce shared morality, to prevent its disintegration","the law has no connection with morality, since the two are wholly separate","the law must never interfere with private conduct between consenting adults","the law should enforce only the commands of a sovereign backed by sanction"],"a":0,"e":"Devlin's view, a response to the Wolfenden Report, was criticised by H.L.A. Hart, who followed Mill's harm principle and argued that immorality alone is not enough to justify legal punishment."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Liberty","q":"The 'harm principle', that the only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over a member of a civilised community against his will is to prevent harm to others, is associated with:","o":["Thomas Hobbes","Jeremy Bentham","John Stuart Mill","John Locke"],"a":2,"e":"Mill's On Liberty sets out this principle as the boundary of State intervention in individual liberty, and it influenced the debate on decriminalising consensual same-sex relations (Navtej Singh Johar)."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Legal Positivism","q":"The 'sources thesis', that the existence and content of law depend on social sources and not on moral argument, is closely associated with:","o":["John Finnis","Ronald Dworkin","Joseph Raz","Lon Fuller"],"a":2,"e":"Raz defended exclusive legal positivism, and argued that law claims authority, which can be identified without moral reasoning."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Corporate Criminal Liability","q":"In Iridium India Telecom Ltd. v. Motorola Inc. (2011), the Supreme Court held that a company:","o":["can be prosecuted for mens rea offences, by the doctrine of attribution","cannot be prosecuted for any offence that requires a guilty mind at all","can be prosecuted only for offences that are punishable with fine alone","can be prosecuted only after its directors have been convicted by a court"],"a":0,"e":"The mens rea of the person in control of the company, the directing mind, is attributed to the company. Standard Chartered Bank v. Directorate of Enforcement (2005) allowed prosecution even where the punishment includes imprisonment, by imposing a fine."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Precedent","q":"In Bengal Immunity Co. v. State of Bihar (1955), the Supreme Court held that Article 141:","o":["binds the Supreme Court absolutely to every one of its earlier decisions","does not bind the Supreme Court itself, which may overrule its decisions","binds only the High Courts, and not the subordinate courts in any State","binds the Supreme Court only for decisions of a Constitution Bench"],"a":1,"e":"The Court overruled State of Bombay v. United Motors, holding that, while it should not lightly depart from its precedents, it is not bound by them if they are manifestly erroneous."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Precedent","q":"If a two-judge bench of the Supreme Court disagrees with the view of a coordinate bench of equal strength, it should:","o":["ignore the earlier decision, as it is of the same strength","seek the opinion of the High Court on the legal question","refer the matter to a larger bench for resolution","overrule the earlier decision of the coordinate bench directly"],"a":2,"e":"Judicial discipline requires that a bench of equal strength is bound by the earlier decision, and can only express doubt and request a larger bench."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'falsus in uno, falsus in omnibus' means 'false in one thing, false in everything', and in Indian courts it is:","o":["a rule applicable only to the testimony of a police officer","a binding rule of law, so the whole testimony must be rejected","a rule applicable only in civil cases, and not in criminal trials","not a rule of law, so the court must sift the true from the false"],"a":3,"e":"Indian courts treat it as a rule of caution, not law, and the evidence of a witness may be accepted in part if it is reliable (Nisar Ali v. State of U.P.)."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Interpretation","q":"The maxim 'expressio unius est exclusio alterius' means:","o":["the exclusion of one thing implies the inclusion of all others","the express mention of one thing excludes others not mentioned","the mention of one thing includes everything of the same kind","every word in a statute must be given a meaning of its own"],"a":1,"e":"It is a rule of statutory interpretation, though courts apply it with caution, as it may lead to injustice when the legislature did not intend an exclusion."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Interpretation","q":"The maxim 'noscitur a sociis' in statutory interpretation means that:","o":["a word is known by the company it keeps","a word is to be read literally and not contextually","a word has the same meaning wherever it appears","a word means whatever the legislature intended"],"a":0,"e":"The meaning of a doubtful word may be gathered from the words associated with it in the provision, and the principle is related to ejusdem generis."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Interpretation","q":"The 'mischief rule' of statutory interpretation originated in:","o":["Heydon's Case (1584)","Pepper v. Hart (1993)","Grey v. Pearson (1857)","Riche v. Ashbury Railway Carriage Co. (1875)"],"a":0,"e":"The rule directs a court to examine the common law before the Act, the mischief the Act was meant to cure, the remedy provided, and the true reason for the remedy, and to interpret the Act to suppress the mischief and advance the remedy."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Interpretation","q":"The rule of 'ejusdem generis' provides that where specific words are followed by general words, the general words are:","o":["read in isolation, without reference to the specific words","ignored altogether, as they add nothing to the specific words","extended to all things, whether of the same kind or not","limited to things of the same kind as the specific words"],"a":3,"e":"For example, in 'cars, buses, trucks and other vehicles', the last words would ordinarily not cover aircraft. The rule applies where the specific words form a class."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Interpretation","q":"In Pepper v. Hart (1993), the House of Lords held that courts may refer to parliamentary materials, such as Hansard, when:","o":["the Government of the day requests the court to do so formally","the legislation is clear, so as to confirm the court's own reading","the legislation has been amended by a later Act of the legislature","legislation is ambiguous, obscure or leads to an absurdity"],"a":3,"e":"The statement relied on must be clear and made by the Minister or promoter of the Bill. Indian courts also refer to legislative history for interpretation, with caution."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Boundaries","q":"The principle of 'uti possidetis juris', as applied in the Frontier Dispute (Burkina Faso v. Mali, 1986), provides that on independence:","o":["new States must renegotiate every boundary with their neighbours afresh","new States may reclaim their pre-colonial territories through use of force","colonial administrative boundaries become the boundaries of the new States","new States become bound by such boundaries only after approval by the UN"],"a":2,"e":"The principle aims to preserve stability and prevent territorial conflicts at the time of decolonisation, and the ICJ described it as a general principle."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Antarctica","q":"The Antarctic Treaty, 1959, which India acceded to in 1983, provides that Antarctica shall be used:","o":["for commercial mining, subject to licences from member States","for military purposes, subject to approval of the Security Council","only by claimant States, to the exclusion of all other nations","for peaceful purposes only, with military activity prohibited"],"a":3,"e":"The Treaty freezes territorial claims and promotes scientific cooperation, while the Madrid Protocol (1991) bans mineral resource activities. India has research stations such as Maitri and Bharati."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Outer Space","q":"Under Article II of the Outer Space Treaty, 1967, outer space, including the Moon and other celestial bodies, is:","o":["open to national appropriation by any State that first occupies the area","not subject to national appropriation by claim of sovereignty or occupation","subject to the sovereignty of the State that launches the first satellite","subject to the sovereignty of the permanent members of the Security Council"],"a":1,"e":"The Treaty treats space as the 'province of all mankind', and prohibits the placing of nuclear weapons or weapons of mass destruction in orbit."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Genocide","q":"The definition of genocide in Article II of the Genocide Convention, 1948 protects which of the following groups?","o":["groups defined by their sexual orientation","political groups and social classes alone","economic and professional groups of persons","national, ethnical, racial or religious groups"],"a":3,"e":"The exclusion of political groups was a deliberate choice at the time of drafting. Genocide requires the intent to destroy such a group, in whole or in part, as such."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"International Criminal Court","q":"Which of the following crimes does NOT fall within the subject-matter jurisdiction of the International Criminal Court under the Rome Statute?","o":["The crime of genocide","The crime of aggression","Terrorism","Crimes against humanity"],"a":2,"e":"The Rome Statute covers genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes and the crime of aggression. Terrorism and drug trafficking were left out, as there was no agreement on their definition."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Asylum","q":"In the Asylum Case (Colombia v. Peru, 1950), the ICJ held that Colombia, which had granted diplomatic asylum to a Peruvian political leader:","o":["had not shown a customary rule letting it qualify the offence unilaterally","had an absolute right to grant diplomatic asylum under customary law","had a right to determine the offence conclusively and bind Peru as well","had a right to safe conduct for the leader from Peru without conditions"],"a":0,"e":"The Court found that the practice of diplomatic asylum in Latin America was not so uniform as to constitute a regional custom binding on Peru."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Environmental Treaties","q":"The Kigali Amendment (2016) to the Montreal Protocol aims at the phase-down of:","o":["hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs)","carbon dioxide emissions","chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs)","methane emissions from agriculture"],"a":0,"e":"Although HFCs do not deplete the ozone layer, they are potent greenhouse gases. India has committed to a phased reduction starting from 2032."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Law of the Sea","q":"Under Article 136 of UNCLOS, the 'Area' (the seabed and ocean floor beyond national jurisdiction) and its resources are the:","o":["exclusive property of the coastal States nearest to it","territory open to appropriation by any State by occupation","property of the permanent members of the UN Security Council","common heritage of mankind"],"a":3,"e":"Activities in the Area are organised and controlled by the International Seabed Authority, which is based in Kingston, Jamaica, for the benefit of mankind as a whole."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Global Governance","q":"At the New Delhi G20 Summit in September 2023, which organisation was admitted as a permanent member of the G20?","o":["the European Free Trade Association","the Association of Southeast Asian Nations","the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation","the African Union"],"a":3,"e":"The African Union joined the European Union as the second regional bloc in the G20, during India's presidency."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Domain Names","q":"In Satyam Infoway Ltd. v. Sifynet Solutions Pvt. Ltd. (2004), the Supreme Court held that domain names:","o":["can be protected by passing off, as they work as business identifiers","have no protection under Indian law, since they are mere web addresses","are protected only if registered as trade marks in the Registry","are protected only against use by foreign companies operating abroad"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that a domain name may have all the characteristics of a trade mark and an action for passing off can be maintained to protect it, even though there was no specific law on domain names."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Copyright","q":"The principle that copyright protects the expression of an idea, and not the idea itself, was applied by the Supreme Court in:","o":["Entertainment Network (India) v. Super Cassette (2008)","Gramophone Co. of India v. Birendra Bahadur Pandey (1984)","Eastern Book Company v. D.B. Modak (2008)","R.G. Anand v. Delux Films (1978)"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that there is no copyright in an idea, a subject matter, themes or plots, but only in the way they are expressed, and the test is whether a spectator gets the unmistakable impression that the work is a copy."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Patents","q":"Under Section 53 of the Patents Act, 1970, the term of a patent is:","o":["fourteen years from the date of filing of the application","twenty years from the date of grant of the patent","twenty years from the date of filing of the application","ten years from the date of publication of the application"],"a":2,"e":"The term is not extendable, and the patent lapses if renewal fees are not paid every year."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Surveillance","q":"In PUCL v. Union of India (1997), the Supreme Court, dealing with telephone tapping under Section 5(2) of the Telegraph Act, held that:","o":["tapping is prohibited in every circumstance, even to meet a threat to security","tapping is allowed only with safeguards, including a review committee","tapping needs only the approval of the officer who conducts the interception","tapping is permissible freely, as it is an executive power of the State"],"a":1,"e":"The Court laid down guidelines, such as authorisation by the Home Secretary, limits on the duration of interception and the constitution of a Review Committee, which were later incorporated in the Telegraph Rules and in the new telecom law."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Consumer Protection","q":"Which of the following is a 'dark pattern' prohibited under the Guidelines for Prevention and Regulation of Dark Patterns, 2023?","o":["Basket sneaking, adding items to the cart without the consumer's consent","Offering a loyalty discount on the next purchase made by the customer","Offering a free-return policy on all of the products sold on the platform","Offering a price match guarantee against rival platforms' listed prices"],"a":0,"e":"Other specified patterns include false urgency, confirm shaming, forced action, subscription traps and drip pricing. The Central Consumer Protection Authority enforces the Guidelines."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Takeovers","q":"Under the SEBI (Substantial Acquisition of Shares and Takeovers) Regulations, 2011, an acquirer must make an open offer on acquiring shares carrying voting rights of:","o":["51 per cent or more of the target company","15 per cent or more of the target company","10 per cent or more of the target company","25 per cent or more of the target company"],"a":3,"e":"An open offer is also triggered by creeping acquisition of more than 5 per cent in a financial year, once an acquirer holds between 25 and 75 per cent, and by acquiring control."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Company Law","q":"Under Section 245 of the Companies Act, 2013, members or depositors may seek relief on behalf of all members against the company's wrongful acts through:","o":["a criminal complaint before the Special Court of the area","a class action suit before the National Company Law Tribunal","a derivative suit before the Securities Appellate Tribunal","a representative suit before the Registrar of Companies"],"a":1,"e":"A class action may be filed by the prescribed number of members or depositors, for example, at least 100 members or those holding 10 per cent of the share capital."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Corporate Law","q":"The rule in Foss v. Harbottle (1843) states that:","o":["directors are personally liable for all the debts of the company","the company is the proper plaintiff for its wrongs, and the majority rules","any shareholder can sue the directors for every wrong done to the company","courts must interfere in all of the internal disputes of a company"],"a":1,"e":"The rule has exceptions, such as fraud on the minority and ultra vires acts, and the Companies Act provides statutory remedies for oppression and mismanagement (Sections 241-242)."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Insolvency","q":"In Lalit Kumar Jain v. Union of India (2021), the Supreme Court upheld the Government's notification applying provisions of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code to:","o":["all individuals having any outstanding loans from banks","partnership firms and limited liability partnerships only","Government companies and public sector undertakings","personal guarantors to corporate debtors"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that approval of a resolution plan does not discharge the personal guarantor of his liability, and the creditor may proceed against him."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Arbitration","q":"In Bharat Aluminium Co. v. Kaiser Aluminium Technical Services (BALCO, 2012), the Supreme Court held that Part I of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996:","o":["applies only to domestic arbitrations involving only government parties","applies to every arbitration, whether it is seated in India or abroad","applies only to arbitrations in which Indian companies are parties","applies only to arbitrations seated in India, and operates prospectively"],"a":3,"e":"The decision overruled Bhatia International and Venture Global, and limited the Indian courts' role in foreign-seated arbitrations. The 2015 amendment partly restored the interim relief role through Section 2(2)."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"GST","q":"In Union of India v. Mohit Minerals Pvt. Ltd. (2022), the Supreme Court held that the recommendations of the GST Council are:","o":["binding on the States, but not on the Union Government","binding on the Union and the States, as an Act of Parliament","not binding on the Union and the States, but of persuasive value","binding on the Union, but not on the State Legislatures"],"a":2,"e":"The Court relied on cooperative federalism, and on the fact that both Parliament and the State Legislatures have simultaneous power to legislate on GST under Article 246A."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Natural Justice","q":"In A.K. Kraipak v. Union of India (1969), the Supreme Court held that the rules of natural justice:","o":["apply only to judicial actions of the courts, and never to administrative ones","apply only where a statute has specifically provided for them in its text","do not apply to selection boards in which the members are all public servants","apply to administrative action too; a board member who is a candidate is disqualified"],"a":3,"e":"The Court blurred the distinction between quasi-judicial and administrative functions, and held that the rule against bias was breached since one member of the selection board was himself a candidate."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Bias","q":"The doctrine of necessity in administrative law allows:","o":["a biased adjudicator to act, where no other authority is competent to decide","a judge to sit in his own cause, if the parties give their consent","any biased adjudicator to decide a case whenever he wishes to do so","an authority to avoid hearing a party, where the time available is short"],"a":0,"e":"The doctrine is an exception to the rule against bias, and applies when the disqualified authority is the only one with jurisdiction, as in the case of a Supreme Court bench deciding the judges' salary."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"RTI","q":"In CPIO, Supreme Court of India v. Subhash Chandra Agarwal (2019), the Constitution Bench held that the office of the Chief Justice of India:","o":["is outside the RTI Act, since it is a constitutional office of the State","is a public authority only for the administrative orders of the courts","is a public authority under the RTI Act, with judicial independence kept","is a public authority only for the purposes of information on salaries"],"a":2,"e":"The Court held that the right to information and the judiciary's independence are not in conflict, and that information such as the Collegium's proceedings may be disclosed after balancing privacy and public interest."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Forest Rights","q":"In Orissa Mining Corporation v. Ministry of Environment and Forest (2013), the Niyamgiri case, the Supreme Court directed that the decision on the Vedanta bauxite mining proposal be made by:","o":["the National Green Tribunal, under the NGT Act, 2010","the Gram Sabhas, under the Forest Rights Act, 2006","the State Pollution Control Board, under the Air Act, 1981","the Central Government alone, through a notification"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the Gram Sabhas should decide on the religious and cultural rights of the Dongria Kondh tribe, and the Gram Sabhas unanimously rejected the proposal."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Air Pollution","q":"The Commission for Air Quality Management in National Capital Region and Adjoining Areas, established by a 2021 law, replaced which earlier body?","o":["the National Green Tribunal, set up under the NGT Act of 2010","the Central Pollution Control Board, set up under the Water Act","the Delhi Pollution Control Committee, set up under the Air Act","the Environment Pollution (Prevention and Control) Authority"],"a":3,"e":"The Commission has the power to give directions and to impose penalties, and has jurisdiction over Delhi and the adjoining States for air quality management."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Disability Rights","q":"In Rajive Raturi v. Union of India (2024), the Supreme Court held that the accessibility standards under the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016:","o":["apply only to new constructions, and not to existing public buildings","are advisory in nature, and need not be followed by the State","are mandatory, and a rule that made them discretionary was invalid","apply only to government buildings, and not to private establishments"],"a":2,"e":"The Court struck down Rule 15 of the 2017 Rules, which diluted the obligation to ensure accessibility, and directed the Union to frame mandatory minimum standards."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Education","q":"The Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education (Amendment) Act, 2019 permits the appropriate Government to allow holding back of a child in:","o":["Class VI and Class X, after the board examination in the school","Class V and Class VIII, after an examination and re-examination","Class IV and Class VII, after the second term examination of the year","Class I and Class III, after the first annual school examination"],"a":1,"e":"The amendment ended the 'no-detention policy' in elementary classes, though the Government may still decide not to hold back any child until the completion of elementary education."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Equal Pay","q":"The principle of 'equal pay for equal work', read into Articles 14 and 16 along with Article 39(d), was applied in:","o":["Randhir Singh v. Union of India (1982)","Francis Coralie Mullin v. Administrator, Delhi (1981)","Bandhua Mukti Morcha v. Union of India (1984)","Air India v. Nargesh Meerza (1981)"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that, although the principle is a Directive Principle, it can be enforced through Articles 14 and 16 where employees doing the same work are paid unequally without reasonable classification."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Manual Scavenging","q":"In Dr. Balram Singh v. Union of India (2023), the Supreme Court directed compensation for death of a person in sewer cleaning of:","o":["₹5 lakh","₹10 lakh","₹30 lakh","₹20 lakh"],"a":2,"e":"Earlier, in Safai Karamchari Andolan v. Union of India (2014), the compensation was fixed at ₹10 lakh. The 2023 ruling also fixed ₹20 lakh for permanent disability, and ₹10 lakh for other disabilities."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Emergency","q":"In 2024, the Government of India declared 25 June of each year as 'Samvidhan Hatya Diwas' to commemorate:","o":["the enactment of the 42nd Amendment in 1976","the adoption of the Constitution in 1949","the imposition of the Emergency in 1975","the lifting of the Emergency in March 1977"],"a":2,"e":"The Emergency was proclaimed under Article 352 on 25 June 1975 on the ground of internal disturbance, and it lasted for 21 months."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Gig Workers","q":"Which State became the first to enact a law for the social security and welfare of platform-based gig workers, in 2023?","o":["Telangana","Karnataka","Maharashtra","Rajasthan"],"a":3,"e":"The Rajasthan Platform Based Gig Workers (Registration and Welfare) Act, 2023 was followed by other States, including Karnataka in 2025. (Verify the position in other States.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Social Security","q":"Under the Code on Social Security, 2020, aggregators are required to contribute to a social security fund for gig and platform workers an amount of:","o":["5 to 10 per cent of annual turnover, with no cap on the contribution","0.5 per cent of annual turnover, with a cap of 1 per cent of turnover","1 to 2 per cent of annual turnover, capped at 5 per cent of worker payments","10 per cent of payments to workers, with no limit based on turnover"],"a":2,"e":"The Code, brought into force in November 2025, for the first time recognises gig and platform workers. (Verify the rates in the final rules.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Data Protection","q":"The Data Protection Board of India under the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 functions as:","o":["a civil court with the powers of a High Court to issue writs","a criminal court that tries all of the offences under the Act","a digital office that inquires into breaches and imposes penalties","an appellate court against all orders of the Supreme Court of India"],"a":2,"e":"The Board is appointed by the Central Government, and appeals against its decisions lie to the Telecom Disputes Settlement and Appellate Tribunal (TDSAT)."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Judiciary","q":"Who is the longest-serving Chief Justice of India, with a tenure of over seven years (1978-1985)?","o":["A.N. Ray","Y.V. Chandrachud","M. Hidayatullah","P.N. Bhagwati"],"a":1,"e":"Justice Y.V. Chandrachud served from 22 February 1978 to 11 July 1985, and his son, D.Y. Chandrachud, later served as the 50th Chief Justice (2022-2024)."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Judiciary","q":"Who was the first woman judge of the Supreme Court of India, appointed in 1989?","o":["Justice M. Fathima Beevi","Justice Gyan Sudha Misra","Justice Ruma Pal","Justice Sujata Manohar"],"a":0,"e":"Fathima Beevi was earlier the first woman judge of the Kerala High Court, and later served as the Governor of Tamil Nadu."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Legal Offices","q":"Who was the first Attorney General of India?","o":["C.K. Daphtary","Soli Sorabjee","M.C. Setalvad","K. Parasaran"],"a":2,"e":"M.C. Setalvad held office from 1950 to 1963. Article 76 provides for the Attorney General, who is the highest law officer of the Government of India."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Legal Services","q":"National Legal Services Day, observed in India to spread awareness on legal aid, is celebrated on:","o":["10 December","26 November","9 November","15 August"],"a":2,"e":"The day marks the enforcement of the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987, which gives statutory shape to Article 39A (free legal aid). Constitution Day is 26 November, and Human Rights Day is 10 December."}],"b":[{"id":"S1","m":25,"w":450,"q":"The Supreme Court has held that Governors cannot indefinitely withhold assent to Bills (State of Punjab, 2023) and has considered time limits for Governors and the President in a Presidential Reference. Discuss the constitutional position on assent to State Bills and its implications for federalism.","o":["Text: Articles 200 and 201; options of assent, withholding with return, reservation for the President.","Case law: Shamsher Singh (aid and advice), State of Punjab v. Principal Secretary to Governor (2023), and the later Presidential Reference on timelines (Verify the latest outcome).","Federal concerns: delay as a de facto veto, Governor as a constitutional head and not a parallel executive, Sarkaria and Punchhi Commission views.","Counter-view: Governor's discretion for Bills repugnant to the Constitution or Union law, and separation of powers limits on judicially imposed timelines.","Conclusion: assent must be timely and reasoned; clear conventions or statutory timelines would protect cooperative federalism."]},{"id":"S2","m":25,"w":450,"q":"Platform and gig work has outgrown the traditional employer-employee framework. Critically examine whether the Code on Social Security, 2020 and recent State laws adequately protect gig workers.","o":["Problem: algorithmic management, lack of employment status, absence of social security and minimum earnings.","Legal framework: Code on Social Security 2020 (gig and platform worker definitions, aggregator contribution of 1-2% of turnover, capped at 5% of payouts); Rajasthan 2023 and Karnataka laws (Verify the current status).","Constitutional anchors: Articles 14, 21, 38, 39 and 43; test of control and economic reality in worker classification.","Gaps: no recognition as employees, limited grievance redress, portability of benefits, enforcement of rules.","Conclusion: a hybrid 'third category' with enforceable minimum standards and transparent algorithms is the way forward."]}]};
